tants and politicians it  wasn't as great at producing engineers  in fact it didn't offer an undergrad  engineering program at all and morris  already knew that he wanted to be an  engineer so after his first year he  transferred to mit and quickly completed  his bachelor's and master's degrees in  mechanical engineering morris was  planning to continue on to do his phd  but he encountered an unexpected hurdle  he failed his qualifying exams in fact  he failed him twice  so unable to continue with his studies  morris went out to look for an  engineering job the semiconductor  industry back then looked absolutely  nothing like today early transistors  were available but integrated circuits  wouldn't be prototyped until the late  1950s and the work was being done in  germanium not silicon it's not clear how  much morris knew about this at the time  he was a mechanical engineer not an  electrical engineer  despite this he accepted an offer from  sylvania electric products which had  just established a semiconductor  division morris worked at sylvania for  three years he worked hard but he  realized the sales team was over  promising and the product team was under  delivering when he heard a senior  manager say that we cannot make what we  sell and we cannot sell what we make he  knew that it was time to move on he'd  heard good things about a company in  texas today if you know about texas  instruments you probably know them as a  manufacturer of graphing calculators but  back then they were a juggernaut they  got their start making devices for  measuring seismic activity for oil  exploration that desire to power the  future of industry led them into  computing and semiconductors in 1958  morris received an offer to join as an  engineering supervisor with  responsibility for a production line it  didn't take long for him to score his  first win the production line that  morris was in charge of made chips for  ibm ibm was working on a product that  they thought would represent a big leap  forward its first mainframe computer it  used transistors instead of vacuum tubes  anticipating high demand ibm partially  outsourced chip production to texas  instruments this was the first iteration  of the basic pattern that would  eventually define morris's career a  company making ambitious hardware  outsourcing chip production to a company  he worked for ibm's own plant was  producing chips with a yield of about 10  percent let's discuss what we mean by  yield in semiconductor production  because this won't be the last time we  discuss it yield refers to the usable  output generated by a production process  in a process as delicate and complex as  semiconductor manufacturing small  defects accumulate into huge error rates  a ten percent yield meant that nine out  of the ten chips that ibm was producing  were failing it got even worse when  morris came on board the yield of his  line it was about zero percent but he'd  acquired some knowledge from three years  at sylvania and his own self-directed  study so like a chef he experimented  with the recipe after enough changes  morris was able to improve the yield of  his line from virtually zero to about 20  percent that's tiny compared to today's  top fabs but it was a big deal for texas  instruments ibm and morris chang  suddenly everyone at texas instruments  knew his name ibm initially thought  morris had gotten lucky but when they  sent engineers to talk to him they  realized this guy knew what he was doing  morris described the theories he'd been  testing and why his experimental process  had worked he wasn't just throwing darts  at a dartboard he was iterating  successfully by 1961 thanks to these  achievements morris had been promoted to  be the manager of a big engineering  section working on germanium transistor  development eager to support their  rising star texas instruments offered to  pay for morris to complete his phd he  accepted the offer and returned to  stanford for two and a half years this  time he passed his qualifying exam with  flying colors a few months after his  return the vice president of the  semiconductor division asked him to head  up an entire product department all of a  sudden morris was a general manager in  charge of more than 2 000 people he was  well and truly on the fast track one of  his signature achievements in his first  gm role was to complete the development  of one of those germanium transistors he  started working on after returning from  stanford texas instruments began  manufacturing that transistor at scale  to sell to its most important client ibm  morris had made another highly lucrative  contribution to the business morris was  moved from less important to more  important departments and by early 1967  he was the general manager of the  integrated circuits department the most  important semiconductor department at  all of texas instruments morris had  already made technological breakthroughs  his next breakthrough would come on the  business side in the late 1960s when  texas instruments or any other  semiconductor company began a new  product line it immediately charged its  customers a lot of money on the face of  it this was rational after all new  products required massive upfront  investment but morris with the forensic  problem-solving style that was rapidly  becoming his trademark realized this  approach wasn't optimal it wasn't  optimal because at this early stage of  production maximizing revenue couldn't  be achieved by increasing the price it  would be achieved by maximizing the  yield how could you maximize yield by  maximizing production volume morris  borrowed from his own experience to  realize that improving yields was a  learning process you needed to solve  novel interconnected difficult problems  he realized that the key to improving  yield was to maximize the opportunities  to find the problem and identify the  solution and the best way to do that was  to make as many chips as possible as  fast as possible basically morris  reasoned you always want maximum  capacity morris hired the boston  consulting group to formalize his  thinking the outcome was learning curve  pricing a model that sets prices low at  first in order to quickly kickstart  manufacturing volume and rapidly  increase production yields remember that  idea because we'll come back to it later  a happy side effect was that by  initially setting the price low texas  instruments was also able to grab lots  of market share morris had made a key  realization one that is still just as  relevant today sales and product aren't  separate silos they're highly  complementary you need a great product  but you need a great business model too  morris's insight and the work of the  boston consulting group made texas  instruments integrated circuits  department the biggest and most  profitable integrated circuits business  in the world  much of the credit belonged to morris he  had learned the technology and business  of this rapidly evolving industry better  than anyone in 1972 texas instruments  rewarded his accomplishments by  promoting him to vice president morris  was now responsible for texas  instruments entire semiconductor  business it capped an incredible rise  for a man who had left his home to come  to the united states when he was just  18. morris remained in that role for six  years until he transferred to the  consumer products division  and although it didn't seem that way at  the time this is where things started  going wrong for morris management could  sense that a market for consumer  electronics was emerging remember how i  said that today texas instruments is  probably best known for calculators this  is the kind of direction the company  wanted to head in the consumer products  department hadn't gotten off to a great  start but morse's bosses figuring that a  good manager would be a good manager  anywhere moved him over to the new  department it was a mistake the products  were different the customers were  different the market was different and  morris couldn't turn things around so  five years after being moved to consumer  products he was transferred to a job as  vp for quality and people effectiveness  it was a staff job basically almost hr  for morris it was a slap in the face  especially given he had essentially  built texas instruments into a world  leader in semiconductors this bad news  was about to be compounded perhaps  because of its focus on trying to turn  around its consumer products division  texas instruments was caught off guard  by a combination of an industry-wide  transition to metal oxide semiconductor  manufacturing leakage of talent and the  sharp response of its biggest  competitors it lost their advantage in  semiconductors the advantage morris had  worked so hard to build he could see the  writing on the wall from being the guy  everyone thought would be the next ceo  morris's career at texas instruments was  basically over he'd been put out to  pasture in 1983 he resigned 30 years of  service over in an instant of course it  wasn't lost on other companies that one  of the world's best semiconductor  engineers was suddenly unemployed  morse's phone started ringing one of the  calls he fielded was from general  instrument they weren't quite as big as  texas instruments but they were still  fortune 500 big they offered him the  role of president and chief operating  officer he accepted and moved to  manhattan but it was a total bust morris  believed he was joining a company  similar to where he'd just been the  reality was very different he quickly  realized the general instrument  functioned more like a private equity  firm than a semiconductor business  instead of growing organically general  instruments business model focused on  mergers acquisitions and either  incorporating the acquired business into  the parent company or flipping them for  a profit so after just one year general  instrument morris decided to leave he  was crestfallen here's a quote from him  describing his mood at the time after  these two setbacks at ti and gi i did  not think that my aspiration to be the  ceo of a major us company was in the  cards he was right just not in the way  he or anyone else could have expected  morris chang was in his mid 50s and had  a reputation no one ever wants former  rising star things weren't totally  terrible he was financially comfortable  but the phone wasn't ringing off the  hook like it was after he left texas  instruments however an opportunity was  about to emerge from an unlikely place  in the 1970s when he was still at texas  instruments morris had traveled to  taiwan a couple of times to meet with  government officials to discuss building  a manufacturing plant they clearly  remembered him because they offered him  the job of president of the industrial  technology research institute itri was  the taiwanese government's effort to  answer one of the most important  questions facing any developing country  how to sustainably raise its standard of  living remember back then taiwan didn't  have the first rate economy it does  today its strengths were in agricultural  products and low value manufactured  goods the government of the day was  committed to moving up the value chain  it knew that in order to accomplish that  goal it would need to develop cutting  edge capabilities and higher value  technologies like semiconductors itri  was established with the mandate to  conduct applied scientific research for  industrial purposes and then spin off  the successful products as private  companies in 1975 taiwan negotiated the  transfer of a semiconductor technology  license from rca a big american  electronics company it was about a  generation behind the cutting-edge  manufacturing technologies of the day  which were dominated by companies like  intel fairchild and texas instruments  but it was a start in fact itri was able  to use this technology to establish and  then spin off taiwan's first ever  semiconductor foundry united  microelectronics corporation or umc it  was against this backdrop that morris  was offered the job at itri it's really  interesting to hear him describe his  reaction he saw it as a completely new  kind of job in a totally different  environment that difference appealed to  him besides morris reasoned he was  financially secure he could afford to  roll the dice so in 1985 morris became  president of itri the early days were  not easy remember morris was a foreigner  he was born in mainland china and he'd  learned everything he knew about  semiconductors in the united states but  he was making progress sharing his  knowledge and transforming itri into a  high-performing organization one day he  was approached by lee kuo ting li was an  incredibly important figure in taiwan's  economic development after more than a  decade as economy minister and finance  minister by the time morris arrived his  remit had shifted to promoting science  and technology in taiwan lee approached  morris with a pitch here was the  thinking morris had lots of experience  managing large semiconductor businesses  taiwan's government wanted to develop  expertise in semiconductors they had the  manufacturing technology which they had  licensed from rca plus they were willing  to write morris what was effectively a  blank check besides as morris put it  this was an offer he couldn't refuse  lee gave him about four days to develop  a business plan no time to waste then in  an interview about his life which he  gave to the computer history museum  morris explained that he began by  reflecting on what they had in taiwan  his conclusion was very blunt they had  very little strength in r d none in  integrated circuit design not much in  sales and marketing and very little in  intellectual property what taiwan did  have however was a potential competitive  advantage in semiconductor wafer  manufacturing morris decided to start a  company that would fit taiwan's  strengths and avoid its weaknesses a  pure play foundry a company that only  made chips there was just one catch  there was no evidence that a market for  a company like this existed but morris  knew this wasn't proof that a market  would never exist in fact his experience  made him think otherwise  he remembered back to his time at texas  instruments where he encountered lots of  integrated circuit designers who were  itching to start their own companies but  couldn't raise the money the reason  investors couldn't envision a  semiconductor company that didn't also  make its own chips  in the 1980s it cost between 50 and 100  million dollars to start a new chip  company most of that was the cost of  manufacturing and those potential  founders who morris had met simply  couldn't raise that kind of funding an  alternative was to contract production  from one of the big players but not only  was that unreliable you were also lining  the pockets of your rivals neither of  these were great options morris thought  that a pure play foundry could be a  solution  by removing the need for every  semiconductor startup to build its own  fab maybe more of these aspiring  designers could actually form their own  companies and then maybe they'd become  tsmc customers he believed hoped really  that a new market would emerge it was a  completely novel idea at the time all  chip manufacturing was integrated the  few companies out there that did focus  on chip design had to fight for table  scraps morris was under no  misapprehensions everyone including him  realized his plan was a long shot but he  believed the concept had merit and he  trusted in his 30 years of experience in  the semiconductor business morris's  bosses who were the bosses of taiwan  remember listen to his presentation okay  they said set up a pure play foundry but  we know it'll take a lot of money we're  only good for half you have to raise the  other half oh and we want you to raise  most of it from big players like intel  or texas instruments talk about a tough  ask morris met with texas instruments  and intel but they couldn't see what he  saw so having exhausted basically every  other option he met with the dutch  electronics manufacturer phillips  phillips agreed to put up 28 of the  capital they also shared some key  information on the 1.5 micron  lithography process which was used by  the major semiconductor companies in the  mid-1980s some local businessmen were  also let's say strongly encouraged to  invest by the taiwanese government in  the end morris succeeded in raising half  the 220 million that tsmc began its life  with tsmc taiwan's semiconductor  manufacturing company began operations  in 1987 with morris chang as its founder  and inaugural ceo for the first four  years of its life tsmc depended on other  companies throwing them small orders the  dregs basically viability let alone  market dominance looked a very long way  away but then something began to change  morris chang was almost washed up it was  a brutal judgment after 30 years on the  leading edge of the semiconductor  industry but we're talking about a  brutal industry one wrong step failing  to turn around the struggling consumer  products division at texas instruments  turned into another with his ill-fated  move to general instruments a lot of  people believed that moving to taiwan to  try and build a semiconductor industry  from the ground up would be his third  strike after all he was already in his  mid-50s and trying to get ahead in a  young man's world all of this is what  makes the second act of morris's career  the one where he turned tsmc from a  moonshot into the world's ninth largest  company and a linchpin of taiwan  national defense so utterly incredible  it's a sequence of events that a movie  scriptwriter would laugh at as i just  mentioned four years in things were  beginning to change for tsmc morris was  working hard evangelizing cajoling and  just generally twisting elbows he spent  a lot of time talking to people who  wanted to start their own semiconductor  companies but previously couldn't  because they didn't have the money you  don't need a fab morris would say will  be your fap it didn't happen right away  technologically tsmc was starting from a  long way behind the industry's heavy  hitters but they succeeded in closing  the gap how before the emergence of tsmc  semiconductor manufacturing was  dominated by integrated device  manufacturers companies that designed  manufactured and sold their own chips  that kind of integration made the idea  of entering the market let alone  dominating it seemed scary for a new  company tsmc didn't go over the wall  they went around it in his 2003 book the  innovator solution the legendary  business strategist clayton christensen  explained that the natural trajectory of  industries was to move from integrated  architectures to modular ones  highly integrated semiconductor  companies by virtue of trying to do  everything were too big to be responsive  they were too big to succeed christensen  went on whereas in the interdependent  world you had to make all of the key  elements of the system in order to make  any of them in a modular world you can  prosper by outsourcing or by supplying  just one element ultimately the  specifications for modular interfaces  will coalesce as industry standards when  that happens companies can mix and match  components from best of breed suppliers  in order to respond conveniently to the  specific needs of individual customers  this is exactly what tsmc did  specifically morris enabled those guys  who he had come across in his previous  life at texas instruments to start  fabulous companies and in the process  engaged tsmc to manufacture the chips  suddenly an industry defined by vertical  integration because of its staggeringly  high capital expenditure and ip  requirements became defined by  horizontal integration morris had  succee