rom  samsung but then samsung decided it  wanted to make its own smartphones  putting it into direct competition with  apple apple hated that the money it was  paying samsung was being used against it  the relationship soured even further  when apple sued samsung for ip theft  that experience led apple to diversify  its supply chain and straight into the  arms of tsmc in september of 2014 apple  launched the iphone 6 which had a  processor fabbed by tsmc on its 20  nanometer process with just one  exception when it flirted with dual  sourcing chips for its a9 processor from  both tsmc and samsung apple has stuck  with tsmc ever since that's especially  remarkable when you consider the  potential risks of being totally reliant  on a single supplier to produce your  single most critical component but then  again tsmc isn't like most suppliers its  determination to meet apple's needs is a  great example of why tsmc enjoys the  huge advantage in chip making that it  does for one thing it changed its own  rollout schedule to fit apple's  relentless annual release cycle every  new generation of the iphone needs an  improved processor that means tsmc needs  to create a new high-yielding production  line every year it achieved that by  creating a modified half-step process  whereby it generated more modest  performance improvements every year  instead of bigger jumps every 18 months  or two years secondly tsmc is one of the  very few companies that can match apple  when it comes to capital investment in  2013 it spent nine billion dollars to  build a fab in tainan city that only  made chips for apple apple has rewarded  tsmc lavishly for its commitment in 2019  apple paid it 8.2 billion dollars almost  a quarter of its annual revenue securing  the partnership with apple was one of  morris's greatest achievements in the  third act of his career satisfied he  again stepped down as ceo in 2013 but  stayed on as chairman tsmc's incredible  scale built on a foundation of trust and  non-competition has concentrated the  chip making industry in the early 2000s  when tsmc finally caught up to the  leading edge of technology with other  semiconductor companies there were more  than 20 companies at the leading edge by  the mid-2010s there were just six today  there are only two firms manufacturing  the cutting edge five nanometer node at  volume tsmc and samsung both are  planning to put a three nanometer node  into volume production by the second  half of 2022 but before any of that  there's another milestone to cover in  mid-2018 morris chang retired for real  this time he was 86 years old it's  almost impossible to describe what he  has achieved the numbers are beyond  comprehension they're mind-blowing in  2020 tsmc announced an operating profit  of 20 billion dollars on 48 billion  dollars of revenue bloomberg estimates  it makes 4 500 in revenue for every  12-inch wafer it manufactures its gross  margins are in the vicinity of 50 it has  90 market share of the current 5  nanometer node it's planning to spend  around 40 billion on capex in 2023 and  yet the numbers somehow don't paint the  full picture tsmc is a story about  globalization it's a story about  taiwan's economic miracle and it is a  story about morris chang a man with one  foot in the east and another in the west  who built the best chip making company  in the world right now it's hard to  imagine tsmc being dislodged from its  perch even if tsmc were to go out of  business tomorrow and for lots of  reasons let's hope it doesn't it would  still deserve to be remembered as one of  the greatest b2b hardware companies ever  not bad for someone who was washed up  almost 40 years ago thanks for watching  if you'd like to see another video like  this just watch my video on nvidia next  [Music]

