t generally twisting elbows he spent  a lot of time talking to people who  wanted to start their own semiconductor  companies but previously couldn't  because they didn't have the money you  don't need a fab morris would say will  be your fap it didn't happen right away  technologically tsmc was starting from a  long way behind the industry's heavy  hitters but they succeeded in closing  the gap how before the emergence of tsmc  semiconductor manufacturing was  dominated by integrated device  manufacturers companies that designed  manufactured and sold their own chips  that kind of integration made the idea  of entering the market let alone  dominating it seemed scary for a new  company tsmc didn't go over the wall  they went around it in his 2003 book the  innovator solution the legendary  business strategist clayton christensen  explained that the natural trajectory of  industries was to move from integrated  architectures to modular ones  highly integrated semiconductor  companies by virtue of trying to do  everything were too big to be responsive  they were too big to succeed christensen  went on whereas in the interdependent  world you had to make all of the key  elements of the system in order to make  any of them in a modular world you can  prosper by outsourcing or by supplying  just one element ultimately the  specifications for modular interfaces  will coalesce as industry standards when  that happens companies can mix and match  components from best of breed suppliers  in order to respond conveniently to the  specific needs of individual customers  this is exactly what tsmc did  specifically morris enabled those guys  who he had come across in his previous  life at texas instruments to start  fabulous companies and in the process  engaged tsmc to manufacture the chips  suddenly an industry defined by vertical  integration because of its staggeringly  high capital expenditure and ip  requirements became defined by  horizontal integration morris had  succeeded in flipping it 90 degrees in  his own words from 1991 onward the  number of fabulous companies started to  mushroom when morris in his role as head  of taiwan's industrial technology  research institute founded tsmc he  promised that unlike the leading  integrated device manufacturers tsmc  would never compete with its customers  that promise which was backed up by the  reality of tsmc's business model ensured  the presence of something which has been  absolutely vital to tsmc's success  trust tsmc knows that it only succeeds  when its customers succeed its very  existence is built on that fundamental  compatibility of objectives the more  fabulous semiconductor companies that  were founded the more and more  businesses tsmc received its flywheel  began to spin in 1994 the year after  opening its third fab tsmc ipod in  taiwan at a 4 billion market cap by the  early 2000s boosted by cornering the  booming market for dedicated graphics  chips tsmc had caught up to its  competitors and surpassed them i want to  take some time to explain how they did  this because this is a really important  part of our story simply put tsmc beat  its rivals and put itself in an almost  unassailable position because it  maximized its opportunities for learning  firstly by making chips for a diverse  array of customers it needed to  constantly solve different types of  problems secondly the more chips they  manufactured the more revenues they  could reinvest in r d today tsmc has one  of the most valuable patent portfolios  in the world third despite being spun  off from taiwan's state-run industrial  research institute it wasn't shielded  from competition morris and his  colleagues knew that the only way to  turn tsmc into a world-class company was  to force it to compete with the world's  best companies last but not least they  are absolutely obsessed with iterative  improvements in its manufacturing  process semiconductor manufacturing is  one of the hardest things to do in the  